{"@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[
{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need a permit to remove a load-bearing wall in Toronto?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Removing or altering a load-bearing wall is structural work and needs a building permit, with drawings that show the new beam, posts and how the load reaches the foundation."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need a permit to remove a non-load-bearing wall?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Often, yes. If the wall contains plumbing, ducts or wiring that will be moved, is part of a fire separation, or affects exits or smoke alarms, a permit is generally needed. Check before you start."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I tell if a wall is load-bearing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Clues include walls that run perpendicular to the joists above, sit over a beam or wall in the basement, or have another wall stacked above them. These are only signs; the wall should be confirmed by a qualified designer or engineer."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does a permit take for a house in Toronto?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The City aims to review a complete application for a house within 10 business days. Incomplete applications take longer, so a complete set of drawings matters."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Who can prepare the drawings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A BCIN-qualified designer, an architect or an engineer, or the homeowner. A structural engineer usually designs longer or engineered beams."}}]}
